## Why this exists (read once, before your first evaluation)

Team artifacts get a team grade, but people, not teams, earn credit — and the instructor can see your repo, not your rooms. This evaluation is how in-team reality reaches the person doing the grading: who carried what, who unblocked whom, who kept the charter's promises. It is deliberately **anchored to your own team charter** — you're evaluating against the standard you all agreed to in October, not against unstated expectations.

Two norms make this work. **Honesty:** inflated evals hide problems until they're expensive, and they shortchange the teammate who actually carried the work. **No retaliation:** scoring someone down for honest feedback they gave you — in review, in the retro, or here — is itself a professionalism failure, and reciprocal-drop patterns are visible in the data. Evaluating colleagues honestly and fairly, in confidence, is something you will do throughout your career: promotion input, 360 reviews, reference calls. This is the practice round with the safety on: peer input **informs** grade adjustments within bounds; it never mechanically sets them, and no one is penalized on peer numbers alone.

